window.NEO_FIELDKIT={"unit":"angles","expedition":"The Architect’s Studio","tools":[{"title":"Protractor","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><path d=\"M10 70 A40 40 0 0 1 90 70 Z\" fill=\"#e6eef5\" stroke=\"#2f4f6b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M50 70 L50 30\" stroke=\"#b0412f\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M50 70 L86 70\" stroke=\"#b0412f\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M50 60 H60 V70\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b0412f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f4f6b\">90°</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>Angles measure turn, in degrees (°).</b></p><p>A full turn is 360°. A right angle, a quarter turn, is 90°.</p><p>Less than 90° is <i>acute</i>; between 90° and 180° is <i>obtuse</i>.</p>","room":"The Drawing Board","lesson":"Angles 1 · Measuring and drawing angles"},{"title":"Compass rose","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"38\" fill=\"#f3ead6\" stroke=\"#8a6d40\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M50 14 L56 50 L50 86 L44 50 Z\" fill=\"#2f4f6b\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"11\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">N</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"99\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">S</text><path d=\"M14 50 H86\" stroke=\"#b0412f\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/></svg>","body":"<p><b>Half a turn is 180°.</b></p><p>Turning from West to East is half a turn: a straight line.</p><p>Angles that sit together on a straight line add up to 180°.</p>","room":"The Compass Rose","lesson":"Angles 2 · Angles on a line and at a point"},{"title":"Set square","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><path d=\"M14 82 L86 82 L14 20 Z\" fill=\"#e8f0e2\" stroke=\"#3f5f45\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M14 72 H24 V82\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3f5f45\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"56\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">+ + = 180</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>Finding a missing number in a total.</b></p><p>40 + 50 + ? = 180: first 40 + 50 = 90, then count on from 90 to 180. The missing number is 90.</p><p>Check by adding all three.</p>","room":"The Set Square","lesson":"Angles 3 · Angles in a triangle"},{"title":"Shape templates","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"20\" width=\"36\" height=\"36\" fill=\"#efe6d2\" stroke=\"#6b5a40\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"54\" y=\"28\" width=\"38\" height=\"22\" fill=\"#e6eef5\" stroke=\"#2f4f6b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M30 64 L44 78 L30 94 L16 78 Z\" fill=\"#e8f0e2\" stroke=\"#3f5f45\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M10 26 H16 V20 M54 34 H60 V28\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b0412f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></svg>","body":"<p><b>Squares and rectangles have four right angles.</b></p><p>A square also has four equal sides. A rectangle's opposite sides are equal.</p><p>A right angle is marked with a small square in the corner.</p>","room":"The Template Drawer","lesson":"Angles 4 · Because–therefore: quadrilaterals"},{"title":"Fence line","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"14\" y=\"26\" width=\"72\" height=\"48\" fill=\"#e8f0e2\" stroke=\"#7a5536\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-dasharray=\"8 4\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">4 m</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">all the way round</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>Perimeter is the distance all the way round.</b></p><p>A rectangle 4 m long and 2 m wide has perimeter 4 + 2 + 4 + 2 = 12 m.</p><p>Add every side, and keep the units.</p>","room":"The Fence Line","lesson":"Area and perimeter 1 · Perimeter or area?"},{"title":"Floor tiles","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><g fill=\"#efe6d2\" stroke=\"#6b5a40\" stroke-width=\"2\"><rect x=\"20\" y=\"28\" width=\"30\" height=\"30\"/><rect x=\"50\" y=\"28\" width=\"30\" height=\"30\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"58\" width=\"30\" height=\"30\"/><rect x=\"50\" y=\"58\" width=\"30\" height=\"30\"/></g><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">rows × columns</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>Area is the space inside, counted in squares.</b></p><p>5 rows of 2 squares is 10 squares: 5 × 2 = 10.</p><p>For a rectangle, multiply the rows by the columns.</p>","room":"The Tiled Floor","lesson":"Area and perimeter 2 · Area of rectangles and triangles"},{"title":"Half card","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"14\" y=\"24\" width=\"72\" height=\"50\" fill=\"#f3ead6\" stroke=\"#8a6d40\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M14 74 L86 24 L86 74 Z\" fill=\"#e6eef5\" stroke=\"#2f4f6b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">half</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>Multiply first, then halve.</b></p><p>Half of 10 × 3: 10 × 3 = 30, and half of 30 is 15.</p><p>Halving the answer is the same as dividing it by 2.</p>","room":"The Sloping Wall","lesson":"Area and perimeter 3 · Parallelograms and trapezia"},{"title":"Tape measure","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"34\" width=\"34\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#d9b25e\" stroke=\"#8f6a26\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"42\" y=\"44\" width=\"52\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#f2d873\" stroke=\"#8f6a26\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M50 44V52M58 44V50M66 44V52M74 44V50M82 44V52M90 44V50\" stroke=\"#3a2a10\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"88\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">1 m = 100 cm</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>1 m = 100 cm.</b></p><p>2 m = 200 cm, and 1.5 m = 150 cm.</p><p>Changing metres to centimetres moves the digits two columns to the left.</p>","room":"The Garden Room","lesson":"Area and perimeter 4 · Composite shapes and design choices"}]};
